### Extensions are a packaging mechanism; C buys you access below the SQL layer

`CREATE EXTENSION` is fundamentally a packaging and versioning mechanism — a control file plus one or more SQL scripts that get bundled, installed, and upgraded as a unit. A pure SQL/PL/pgSQL extension is just a set of functions, types, and objects defined in ordinary SQL, wrapped so they can be installed with one command and cleanly uninstalled with `DROP EXTENSION`. This covers a huge fraction of real-world extension needs (a library of business-logic functions, a set of custom domains and triggers) with no C required at all.

A C extension additionally compiles to a shared library (`.so`) loaded into the backend process, giving it access to PostgreSQL's internal C APIs — this is what's required for anything that needs to hook into the engine below what SQL/PLpgSQL can express: implementing a new index access method (like `pgvector`'s IVFFlat/HNSW), a new data type with custom binary representation and operators evaluated in native code for performance (not just a domain wrapping an existing type), a background worker process, or hooks into the planner/executor itself.

```c
// Minimal C function callable from SQL
#include "postgres.h"
#include "fmgr.h"
PG_MODULE_MAGIC;

PG_FUNCTION_INFO_V1(add_two_ints);
Datum add_two_ints(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS) {
    int32 a = PG_GETARG_INT32(0);
    int32 b = PG_GETARG_INT32(1);
    PG_RETURN_INT32(a + b);
}
```
```sql
-- extension_name--1.0.sql
CREATE FUNCTION add_two_ints(int, int) RETURNS int
AS 'MODULE_PATHNAME', 'add_two_ints'
LANGUAGE C STRICT;
```

The justification threshold is roughly: reach for C only when the work is either (a) fundamentally impossible from SQL/PLpgSQL — implementing an index access method, a new physical data type with custom on-disk representation — or (b) a hot-path computation where the interpretation overhead of PLpgSQL genuinely dominates runtime (tight numeric loops processing millions of values). For business logic, even complex business logic, PLpgSQL's overhead is rarely the bottleneck compared to I/O and query execution, so C adds real cost (memory-unsafe code running inside the database backend process — a crash in a C extension can crash or corrupt the whole PostgreSQL server, unlike a PLpgSQL exception which is safely contained) without matching benefit.

**Common pitfall:** reaching for a C extension for performance reasons without first establishing that PLpgSQL interpretation overhead is actually the bottleneck (rather than, say, a missing index or a bad query plan) — teams sometimes invest significant engineering time writing and maintaining C code, taking on real crash-safety risk to the whole server process, for a function whose actual runtime was dominated by a sequential scan the whole time, and a C rewrite of the function itself changes nothing about the underlying query plan.

